ha-ani-weather-card — derived from, not bundled
-----------------------------------------------

Every component named above is an npm dependency compiled into the bundle.
This one is not, and the distinction is the point of this entry: the Animated
Weather widget was written by reading ha-ani-weather-card
(https://github.com/alexsto1an/ha-ani-weather-card), Copyright (c) 2025
Animated Weather Card, distributed under the MIT License, and porting parts of
it. No file of that project is copied in or linked against. That card is Lit
and GSAP and emits a stylesheet; the Dashable widget draws React and Framer
Motion, and the rendering, the motion budget and the element caps are Robot One
Software's own. What was ported is substantial enough that the MIT notice below
travels with it:

  * the OKLCH sky palette tables — a colour set for each weather condition at
    each time of day — the dawn / day / dusk / night phase derivation built on
    them, the OKLCH-to-sRGB conversion, and the compositing and contrast maths.
    From the card's src/sky.ts, into src/lib/animatedWeather/sky.ts.

  * the two-stop bloom around the light source — a tight core inside a wide
    halo at one origin, flat out to the rim before it falls away. From the
    card's src/fc-css.ts, into src/lib/animatedWeather/sky.ts (SKY_GLOW and
    glowStops, for the sky) and src/lib/animatedWeather/celestial.ts (the bloom
    drawn around the sun and the moon). The reach, the alpha and the pulse are
    re-derived against Dashable's own contrast sweep.

  * the weather-condition classification (from src/utils.ts, getWeatherClass)
    and the readable condition names (from src/weather-data.ts), both into
    src/lib/animatedWeather/motion.ts.

  * the cloud silhouette — a body with a larger puff up and left and a smaller
    one up and right, each translucent so the overlaps read denser — and the
    idea of spreading the clouds across three parallax depths. From the card's
    cloudShape(), into src/lib/animatedWeather/motion.ts (CLOUD_ASPECT,
    CLOUD_PARTS and CLOUD_DEPTHS). Dashable composes the three parts as
    gradients in one node rather than three, and uses a narrower speed spread.

  * the blowing leaves: the palette, the size range, the twirl range, the leaf
    shape and the three gust characters. From the card's createGSAPLeaf in
    src/gsap-effects.ts, into src/lib/animatedWeather/motion.ts (LEAF_COLORS,
    LEAF_SIZE_PX, LEAF_SPIN_DEG) and
    src/components/dashboard/animated-weather/AnimatedWeatherSky.tsx (the
    shape, and the gusts in rollLeaf). Dashable draws them as canvas particles
    rather than as DOM nodes on GSAP timelines.

  * the rain splash and rebound geometry — two droplets thrown out sideways on
    a parabola, and the drop itself springing back up an arc — together with
    the share of drops that splash rather than falling through. From the card's
    createSplash and createRebound, into src/lib/animatedWeather/motion.ts
    (splashAt and RAIN_SPLASH_SHARE).

  * the discipline of computing the weather note's facts on the client — when
    rain starts and stops, the high and the low and when each lands, gusts, UV
    — so that a language model only phrases figures it has been handed and is
    never asked to invent a time or a temperature. From src/note-generator.ts,
    into src/lib/animatedWeather/facts.ts. The four voice names the note offers
    come from the same file, into src/lib/weatherNote.types.ts.

